[38;5;252m[1m## One real bug found and fixed[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;10mprintHexCharLine[0m diverged on callers that leave the upper bits of the argument register dirty. C narrows the [38;5;10mchar[0m parameter in the callee ([38;5;10mmov %edi,%eax; mov %al,-0x4(%rbp); movsbl -0x4(%rbp),%eax[0m), but rustc assumes an [38;5;10mi8[0m parameter was already narrowed by the caller and forwarded the whole register ([38;5;10mmov %edi,%esi[0m). Calling the symbol with [38;5;10m0x1FF[0m gave [38;5;10mffffffff[0m from C and [38;5;10m1ff[0m from Rust.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Fix: the exported wrapper takes a [38;5;10mc_int[0m and truncates to [38;5;10mc_char[0m itself, which now emits [38;5;10mmovsbl %dil,%esi[0m. This stays ABI-compatible with well-formed [38;5;10mchar[0m callers. Everything else in the translation was already correct, including the two subtle spots — the [38;5;10mchar[0m overflow in [38;5;10mbad()[0m ([38;5;10m127*2[0m wrapping to [38;5;10m-2[0m, printed as [38;5;10mfffffffe[0m via the varargs int promotion) and the dead store [38;5;10mdata = ' '[0m in [38;5;10mgoodB2G[0m that must not affect the range check.[0m[0m
